    "summary": "Epic Here Comes Sunshine jam & soaring Truckin' > Other One suite",
    "blurb": "The Tampa show delivers a scorching Here Comes Sunshine and a seamless Truckin' → Nobody's Fault But Mine → The Other One chain. Jerry’s guitar shines with flawless tone while Phil lays down bomb‑heavy bass lines. Overall the performance is tight, jazzy and high‑energy throughout both sets.",
    "review": "December 19, 1973 at Curtis Hixon Convention Hall captures the Dead in peak late‑'73 form – a band that was simultaneously tight and exploratory. The first set builds momentum with a radiant Here Comes Sunshine that bursts into one of the era’s most memorable jams, while Candyman and Big Railroad Blues showcase Keith’s daring piano textures. The second set is where the show truly soars: Weather Report Suite flows into Let It Grow before exploding into an unstoppable Truckin' → Nobody's Fault But Mine → The Other One suite, capped by a wild Keith‑Godchaux outro that fans describe as “air‑piano” brilliance. Jerry’s red‑knob tone glides effortlessly through each transition, Phil’s bass provides both thunderous drive and experimental noise bursts, and Bill Kreutzmann’s snare locks the high‑energy moments in place. For anyone looking to hear a quintessential late‑'73 performance with crystal‑clear soundboard detail, this show is a must‑listen.",
